    "body": "### Problem\n\nIn `pwsh` ( 6 and over ) and `powershell` ( 5.1 ), `echo` is an alias for `Microsoft.PowerShell.Utility\\Write-Output`. It has a [mandatory](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/write-output?view=powershell-7.6#-inputobject) parameter for the output information. This causes the `:messages` window to show the prompt.\n\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/510f61555b9da5800ccd13de2336c90a64c0b9a5/runtime/lua/vim/health/health.lua#L289\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```lua\n  vim.o.shell = 'pwsh'\n  -- Output Encoding set in the `$PROFILE`\n  vim.o.shellcmdflag = '-NoLogo -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Command $PSStyle.OutputRendering = \\'PlainText\\';'\n  -- Required otherwise default is \\\"\n  vim.o.shellquote = ''\n  vim.o.shellxquote = ''\n  vim.fn.system('echo')\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n```lua\nvim.fn.system('echo 1')\n```\n\nThis would be the easiest fix and would translate easily enough between `cmd.exe`, `bash`, `nu`, `zsh`, etc.\n\nOne technical wrinkle is that `echo` in _PowerShell_ is an alias so the most precise execution would be below. Many of the other shells `echo` is a built-in or binary so _PowerShell_ would be the most fragile. Having a check for `vim.o.shell` would complicate the `code` more. It is more defensive and one would support machines where the `echo` alias was removed or the like. FWIW, the `shell-powershell` docs specify `-NoProfile` which does guard against that removal. I run my profile though because I need my aliases and have my interactive `code` checking for a particular library load to signal the process is interactive. Also, I can't think of any reason to remove `echo` though I could see overriding it adding new or different behavior that `vim.health` may not expect.\n```lua\nvim.fn.system('Microsoft.PowerShell.Utility\\\\Write-Output -InputObject 1')\n```\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.4 Build type: Release LuaJIT 2.1.1774638290 Run \"nvim -V1 -v\" for more info\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nN/A\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nN/A\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nN/A\n\n### Installation\n\n`scoop`",
